== To The Reader

So much has been written lately about the times that spiritual discourses are now almost out of season. Men’s minds are so hurried up and down that, it is to be feared, they are much too unsettled to think seriously, as they ought, about their eternal concerns. Alas! Christians have lost much of their communion with Christ and his saints — the heaven upon earth — while they have woefully disputed away the life of religion and the power of godliness, and drained the spirit out of them, into dry and sapless controversies about the government of church and state. So, to recover your spiritual relish for savory practical truths, these sermons of that excellent man of God, of precious memory, are published. In them you are presented:

#[
#set enum(numbering: "I.")
+ With an invitation to a great and wonderful feast, the marriage feast of the Lamb. A feast to wonder at indeed, in which Jesus Christ, the eternal Son of God, is the bridegroom, and where every believer who has been “clothed with the Lord Jesus” (Rom 13:14), “the wedding clothes” (Matt 22:11), is not only the guest but the spouse of Christ, and the bride at this wedding supper. Here Jesus Christ is the master of the feast, and the food and provision too. He is “the Lamb of God” (John 1:29), “the ram caught in the thicket” (Gen 22:13). He is the “fattened calf” (Luke 15:23). When he was sacrificed, “wisdom killed her beasts” (Prov 9:2). At his death, “the oxen and fattened cattle have been killed” (Matt 22:4). Ἀληθῶς βρῶσις καὶ ἀληθῶς πόσις. His flesh is “real food,” and his blood is “real drink” (John 6:55). And so that you may be fully delighted at this feast, Christ is “a rose of Sharon,” “a lily of the valley” (Song 2:1). He is “a sachet of myrrh” (Song 1:13), “a cluster of henna blossoms” (Song 1:14); his name is “perfume poured out” (Song 1:3), and his love is “more delightful than wine” (Song 1:2). In Christ “everything is ready” (Matt 22:4), for “Christ is all and is in all” (Col 3:11). And great is the feast that Christ makes for believers, for it is the marriage feast that the great King makes, “a wedding banquet for his son” (Matt 22:2), the great design and aim of the gospel being to exalt the Lord Jesus Christ, and to give “Him the name above all names” (Phil 2:9). Great is the company that is invited (Luke 14:16), Jews and Gentiles. God keeps open house: “Come, all you#super[+] who are thirsty” (Isa 55:1), and “whoever desires, let him come and take the water of life freely” (Rev 22:17). Great is the fare that is provided. Every guest here has Asher’s portion, “royal delicacies” and “bread of fatness” (Gen 49:20). Here is wine, all excellent, the best, “wines on the lees well refined” (Isa 25:6). Here are “fat things,” indeed, “fat things full of marrow.” Here is “the hidden manna” (Rev 2:17), “the water of life” (Rev 22:17), and the fruit of “the tree of life in the Paradise of God” (Rev 2:7). All that is at this feast is of the best, indeed, the best of the best. Here is variety and plenty too; here is “bread enough and to spare” (Luke 15:17). The feasts of Caligula and Heliogabalus, who ransacked earth, air, and sea to furnish their tables, were nothing to this. And above all, here is welcome for every hungry, thirsty soul; he who bids you come will bid you welcome; “he will not say, ‘Eat,’ when his heart is not with you” (Prov 23:7). The invitation is free, the preparation great, and the hospitality at this feast, suiting the magnificence of the great King, is full and bountiful. All this is treated at length in these excellent sermons, which are therefore deservedly entitled The Marriage Feast between Christ and his Church. We read of a philosopher who had prepared an excellent treatise on happiness and presented it to a great king, and the king answered him, “Keep your book to yourself; I am not at leisure now.” Here is an excellent treasure put into your hand; do not answer us, “I am not at leisure now.” Oh, do not let Christ stand “knocking at your heart, who will come and sup with you” (Rev 3:20), and bring his food with him. Oh, do not let a “deluded heart” any longer “lead you astray” to “feed on ashes” (Isa 44:20); feed no longer on “the pods the pigs were eating” (Luke 15:16), while you may be filled and satisfied with “bread in your Father’s house” (Luke 15:17).

  But this is not all. If you will be pleased to read this book through, you will find that many other useful, timely, and excellent subjects are handled in it besides the marriage feast.

+ Jesus Christ has not only provided a feast, but because he desires that all those for whom it is provided should come to it (which only those who believe do), he takes away the veil of ignorance and unbelief from their hearts. And here you#super[+] will find that this skillful preacher has excellently discussed what this veil is, how it lies by nature upon all, and how it is removed only by the Spirit of Christ. And if the Lord has “destroyed this covering from your heart,” we do not doubt that the truth of this heavenly doctrine will shine comfortingly into your soul.
+ To make his bounty and mercy appear further in this feast, Jesus Christ has given his guests “the bread of life,” and has secured them from the fear of death. They need not fear. There is no mors in ollâ (death in the pot) at this feast. We may feast without fear. Jesus Christ by his “tasting of death has swallowed it up in victory” (1 Cor 15:54). Christ does not make his people such a feast as Dionysius the tyrant is reported once to have made for his flatterer Damocles: he set him at a princely table, but hung a drawn sword by a small thread over his head. But Christ would have us triumph over the king of fears, who was slain by the death of Christ, and by it we are delivered from the bondage of the fear of death (Heb 2:14, 15).

  At other feasts in old times they used to have a death’s head served in among the other dishes, to remind them of their mortality in the midst of all their mirth (a practice of the heathen that condemns the ranting jollity of some loose professors of religion in these times). Κατῆλθεν εἰς θάνατον ἀθάνατος, καὶ τῷ θανάτῳ καθεῖλε θάνατον \[The immortal went down into death, and by death destroyed death\]. But here Christ serves in death’s head, as David did “the head of Goliath” (1 Sam 17:54), the head of a slain and conquered death. Our Samson by his own death “has destroyed death, and has so ransomed us from the power of the grave, and has redeemed us from death” (Hos 13:14), and from the slavish fear of it. All this is handled at length in these following sermons for your comfort and joy, so that you may triumph in his love, through whom you are more than a conqueror.

+ Because “it is a merry heart that makes a continual feast” (Prov 15:15), and so that this feast might be a feast day indeed to your soul, Christ here promises “to wipe away all tears from the faces of his people” (Isa 25:8). The gospel has comforts enough to make glad the hearts of the saints and people of God. “The light of God’s countenance” will refresh them with “inexpressible and glorious joy” (1 Pet 1:8), in the midst “of the valley of the shadow of death” (Ps 23:4). A truly godly person can weep for his sins, however much the world smiles on him; and however much he is afflicted in the world, yet he can and will “rejoice in the God of his salvation” (Hab 3:18). In these sermons you have this gospel promise sweetly opened and applied, and in them you will find directions on when and for what to mourn and weep, and the blessedness of all true mourners, whose “grief will turn to joy” (John 16:20).
+ In these sermons you#super[+] will further find that, though Jesus Christ regards his people highly and welcomes them bountifully, yet they meet with coarse treatment in the world, which is accustomed to revile them as “fools” and “madmen,” as “seditious rebels,” “troublers of Israel,” “proud and hypocritical persons.” But blessed are those who do not “stumble over the stumbling stone” (Rom 9:32), who wear “the reproaches of Christ as their crown,” and “by doing good … silence the ignorance of foolish men” (1 Pet 2:15). For let the world load them with all its revilings, yet “the Spirit of glory … rests on” them (1 Pet 4:14), and in due time he will roll away their reproach, and “bring forth their righteousness like the dawn, their justice like the noonday sun” (Ps 37:6).
+ And because a Christian here has more in hope than in hand, more in what is to come than in possession, “walks by faith” rather than by sense, and lives “on every word that comes from the mouth of God,” and not “on bread alone” (Matt 4:4), you have here, Christian reader, a sweet discourse on the precious promises of Christ, which he has left us here to stay the stomach of the soul till we come to that feast of feasts in heaven: so that by this glimpse we might know in part the greatness of “the glory to be revealed” (1 Pet 5:1); so that the first fruits might be a pledge of the harvest, and the Spirit, “the pledge of our inheritance” (Eph 1:14), a guarantee of that full reward we shall have in heaven, where we shall be brimful of those “eternal pleasures” that are at God’s “right hand” forever (Ps 16:11). Christ has given us promises to uphold our faith and hope till faith is perfected in full enjoyment and hope ends in vision, till Jesus Christ, who is here the object of our faith, is the reward of our faith forever.
+ Now because the comfort of the promises is grounded in the faithfulness of him who has promised, this godly and learned man has strongly asserted the divine authority of the holy Scriptures, proving that they are θεόπνευστοι \[breathed out by God\], that they are the very word of God, that they are αὐτόπιστοι \[worthy of belief in themselves\] and ἀξιόπιστοι, worthy of all acceptance and belief for their own sakes: a truth very fitting for these times, as an antidote to guard you against the poisonous errors of blasphemous anti-scripturists.
+ Lastly, because God often takes a long time to perform the promise, you will find the doctrine of waiting on God excellently handled here: a duty that we earnestly commend to your practice, as suited to these sad times. Say, oh, say with the church, “In the path of Your judgments, O #smallcaps[Lord], we have waited for You” (Isa 26:8); and with the prophet, “I will wait for the #smallcaps[Lord], who is hiding His face from the house of Jacob. I will look for Him” (Isa 8:17). And rest assured that “none of the descendants of Jacob will seek him in vain” (Isa 45:19); he will not “disappoint their hope, nor make the faces of those who wait for him ashamed” (Isa 49:23).
]

Thus we have given you#super[+] a short view of the whole, a brief sum of the treasure these sermons contain. We need say nothing of the author; his former labors “speak sufficiently for him in the gates” (Prov 31:31); his memory is highly honored among the godly and learned. He who enjoys the glory of heaven does not need the praises of men on earth. If anyone should doubt these sermons, as if they were not truly his whose name they bear, let him only observe the style, and the excellent and spiritual matter contained in them, and he will, we hope, be fully satisfied. Besides, there are many ear-witnesses still living who can clear them of any shadow of imposture. They come out without any alteration, except only that some repetitions (which the pulpit bore well) are omitted here. May the Lord make these, and all the other labors of his servants, profitable to his church. And may the Lord so “destroy the veil” from your heart that you may believe, and by faith come to this feast, the joy and comfort of which may swallow up all the slavish fear of death, dry up your tears, and roll away all reproach. And may the Lord give you a waiting heart, to stay your soul on the name of the Lord, to believe his word and his faithful promises, so that in due time you may “rejoice in the God of your salvation.” This is the earnest prayer of

#v(1em)

